1
00:01:22,583 --> 00:01:24,343
Here we are, sir. - Thank you.

2
00:01:24,484 --> 00:01:26,009
You're welcome.

3
00:01:29,056 --> 00:01:30,285
There's the boat.

4
00:01:31,692 --> 00:01:33,661
Those are the three
people I'm supposed

5
00:01:33,827 --> 00:01:36,763
to match wits with all weekend?

6
00:01:36,930 --> 00:01:38,125
Come on, dad.

7
00:01:38,298 --> 00:01:39,994
Trust us.

8
00:01:40,634 --> 00:01:42,474
Now, let's remember,
these three people

9
00:01:42,636 --> 00:01:47,301
donated ten thousand
dollars apiece to charity

10
00:01:47,474 --> 00:01:50,205
to have the privilege of trying
to out-sleuth you this weekend.

11
00:01:50,377 --> 00:01:52,608
Hi. JANE: Hi.

12
00:01:52,779 --> 00:01:54,475
I bet they're all great
big fans of yours.

13
00:01:54,648 --> 00:01:56,617
- Ah, sure.
- So you better be nice.

14
00:01:56,783 --> 00:02:00,015
Yeah, yeah, sure.

15
00:02:00,187 --> 00:02:03,646
Dad, come on, don't you
ever wanna have any fun?

16
00:02:04,691 --> 00:02:07,388
Lot of places I'd rather be.

17
00:02:08,795 --> 00:02:10,491
Hi. MAN: Hi.

18
00:02:11,431 --> 00:02:15,630
Hi, I'm Jane Kravitz. Heh.
This is such a thrill for me.

19
00:02:15,802 --> 00:02:17,828
When my husband
asked me what I wanted

20
00:02:18,005 --> 00:02:20,565
for my birthday this year, this
was the only thing on my list.

21
00:02:20,741 --> 00:02:22,437
- Oh.
- I'm glad you're here.

22
00:02:22,609 --> 00:02:25,078
Oh, thank you. I'm
glad I'm here too.

23
00:02:25,245 --> 00:02:26,304
I'm a writer.

24
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:28,244
- Oh, murder mysteries?
- Well, so far

25
00:02:28,415 --> 00:02:29,781
- just cookbooks.
- Oh.

26
00:02:29,950 --> 00:02:31,714
But I'd like to write mysteries.

27
00:02:31,885 --> 00:02:34,912
Elliot Ross, Titus
Green $ Damien.

28
00:02:35,088 --> 00:02:37,568
- Oh, are you a lawyer?
- Stockbroker. But a mystery buff.

29
00:02:38,091 --> 00:02:40,651
A big mystery buff. Fair
warning, Mr. Matlock.

30
00:02:40,827 --> 00:02:42,467
I intend to give you
a run for your money.

31
00:02:44,197 --> 00:02:45,495
Hi. Hugo Larson. MATLOCK: Hi.

32
00:02:45,666 --> 00:02:48,226
Used to be an investigator
with the DA's office up in Raleigh.

33
00:02:48,402 --> 00:02:50,234
- Oh.
- Took a bullet in the leg last year.

34
00:02:50,404 --> 00:02:52,737
- Had to retire.
- Oh, yeah, well, I guess so.

35
00:02:52,906 --> 00:02:55,705
- But you're still a mystery buff?
- No, sir, always hated it.

36
00:02:55,876 --> 00:02:58,937
- That's why I'm good at solving them.
- Oh. This is my daughter,

37
00:02:59,112 --> 00:03:00,637
Leanne, and Cliff Lewis.

38
00:03:00,814 --> 00:03:02,734
Uh, I'm not his daughter.
- I know.

39
00:03:02,883 --> 00:03:04,112
- We work together.
- Yeah.

40
00:03:04,284 --> 00:03:07,345
In fact, it was pretty much Leanne's
idea to put this whole thing together.

41
00:03:09,056 --> 00:03:10,256
Ah. JANE: Here we go.

42
00:03:10,357 --> 00:03:13,122
Robert Gateway's
own very private island.

43
00:03:15,829 --> 00:03:17,821
There you go. Whoa.

44
00:03:22,969 --> 00:03:25,700
We're supposed to go inside.
Mr. Gateway will meet us there.

45
00:03:25,872 --> 00:03:28,467
There goes the boat.
- Some house.

46
00:03:28,642 --> 00:03:30,322
Wow. CLIFF: You
know what they say.

47
00:03:30,477 --> 00:03:32,207
Robert Gateway makes
more money in one day

48
00:03:32,379 --> 00:03:34,371
than most people make
in an entire lifetime.

49
00:03:34,548 --> 00:03:36,414
It's true, and that's
just from the royalties

50
00:03:36,583 --> 00:03:38,745
on those electronic
games he invented.

51
00:03:38,919 --> 00:03:40,751
Which doesn't count
theme park and the stores

52
00:03:40,921 --> 00:03:43,254
and all the other
stuff he's got going.

53
00:03:49,730 --> 00:03:51,596
Mr. Gateway.

54
00:03:51,998 --> 00:03:54,160
Hello.

55
00:03:55,836 --> 00:03:57,771
Mr. Gateway.

56
00:03:59,706 --> 00:04:01,174
Hmm.

57
00:04:02,375 --> 00:04:04,571
Oh. That's kind of strange.

58
00:04:07,414 --> 00:04:11,317
Something's
wrong. I can feel it.

59
00:04:16,723 --> 00:04:18,749
Mr. Gateway.

60
00:04:20,827 --> 00:04:24,389
- Is that him?
- Looks like it.

61
00:04:30,203 --> 00:04:33,230
- He's dead.
- Oh, my.

62
00:04:34,074 --> 00:04:37,636
All right. Let's not overreact.
Don't touch anything.

63
00:04:37,811 --> 00:04:41,043
Dad, I think you
should call the police.

64
00:04:42,949 --> 00:04:44,474
Oh...

65
00:04:47,387 --> 00:04:49,618
Line's dead. JANE: No.

66
00:04:50,157 --> 00:04:51,853
<i>Hello, there.</i>

67
00:04:53,460 --> 00:04:55,258
<i>Welcome, fellow mystery lovers.</i>

68
00:04:57,697 --> 00:04:59,859
<i>I have some good news.</i>

69
00:05:00,100 --> 00:05:02,535
<i>And I have some bad.</i>

70
00:05:02,702 --> 00:05:07,663
<i>The bad news is, if you're
seeing this, that means I'm dead.</i>

71
00:05:07,841 --> 00:05:11,403
<i>The good news is, I'm pretty
sure I know who killed me.</i>

72
00:05:11,578 --> 00:05:13,774
<i>Now, I could just tell you.</i>

73
00:05:13,947 --> 00:05:17,213
<i>But, then, again, you, you
did donate all of that money.</i>

74
00:05:17,384 --> 00:05:19,114
<i>So why don't we
have a little fun?</i>

75
00:05:19,286 --> 00:05:24,953
<i>I'm sure you'll find solving
my murder is as easy as ABC.</i>

76
00:05:26,693 --> 00:05:29,561
"Fun." I don't think
murder's any fun.

77
00:05:29,729 --> 00:05:31,027
That is so sick.

78
00:05:31,198 --> 00:05:34,168
I mean, what kind of man would
want you to solve his own murder.

79
00:05:34,334 --> 00:05:35,358
I'm leaving.

80
00:05:35,535 --> 00:05:37,527
I, uh, hope that you're
a good swimmer,

81
00:05:37,704 --> 00:05:38,831
because that phone's dead,

82
00:05:39,005 --> 00:05:41,201
and the boat's not coming
back till Sunday night.

83
00:05:42,375 --> 00:05:44,571
"ABC." The dictionary.

84
00:05:44,744 --> 00:05:47,441
He said he left a clue
for us in the dictionary.

85
00:05:47,614 --> 00:05:49,048
Here.

86
00:05:53,987 --> 00:05:55,387
Nothing.

87
00:05:57,290 --> 00:05:58,986
What's that?

88
00:06:15,308 --> 00:06:17,243
"Very good. Or,
should I say, well done.

89
00:06:17,410 --> 00:06:19,936
You are now hot
on my killer's trail."

90
00:06:22,215 --> 00:06:25,811
- Fire place?
- Well done. The oven.

91
00:06:25,986 --> 00:06:28,546
Come on, let's find the kitchen.

92
00:06:32,425 --> 00:06:33,518
Empty.

93
00:06:33,693 --> 00:06:35,628
What about the microwave?

94
00:06:36,997 --> 00:06:38,625
Aha.

95
00:06:39,366 --> 00:06:40,959
"Very good. But
you'd better hurry.

96
00:06:41,134 --> 00:06:44,161
"The scent is getting
weaker and weaker."

97
00:06:44,337 --> 00:06:49,139
Yeah. But look how he spelled weaker,
w-e-e-k-e-r, instead of w-e-a-k-e-r.

98
00:06:49,309 --> 00:06:50,675
"Weeker."

99
00:06:51,711 --> 00:06:54,476
Calendar? MATLOCK: Huh.

100
00:06:58,585 --> 00:07:01,248
Every page is for April.

101
00:07:02,656 --> 00:07:04,989
- April showers?
- April fools?

102
00:07:05,492 --> 00:07:07,154
April fools?

103
00:07:13,900 --> 00:07:15,596
What's going on?

104
00:07:15,769 --> 00:07:18,864
Just getting the old
juices flowing. That's all.

105
00:07:22,275 --> 00:07:26,144
I'm Robert Gateway.
Anyone hungry?

106
00:07:28,515 --> 00:07:31,451
Knew it all the time.
I should've known it.

107
00:07:32,886 --> 00:07:36,482
A man announces his own death
right on TV doesn't make sense.

108
00:07:36,656 --> 00:07:38,750
Didn't you notice how
fast I got to the body?

109
00:07:38,925 --> 00:07:40,587
- I noticed.
- Ha, ha.

110
00:07:40,760 --> 00:07:43,229
So when do the
real festivities begin?

111
00:07:43,396 --> 00:07:47,356
Well, I think tomorrow morning,
maybe early, maybe late.

112
00:07:47,534 --> 00:07:48,934
I'm not telling.

113
00:07:49,102 --> 00:07:51,697
Meantime, if anybody
needs me, I'll be in the den.

114
00:07:52,973 --> 00:07:54,965
Oh, and there ain't nobody
here but us chickens.

115
00:07:55,141 --> 00:07:58,134
So that means everybody
cleans up after his or herself.

116
00:07:58,311 --> 00:07:59,836
See you in the morning.

117
00:08:00,013 --> 00:08:02,278
- Good night. CLIFF:
See you tomorrow.

118
00:08:02,449 --> 00:08:05,419
Well, my leg's starting to
talk to me pretty good, ah.

119
00:08:05,585 --> 00:08:09,647
I'm gonna take care of
these and then get to bed.

120
00:08:09,823 --> 00:08:11,348
- Good night.
- I'll call my husband

121
00:08:11,524 --> 00:08:14,050
and tell him I'm definitely
getting my money's worth.

122
00:08:14,227 --> 00:08:17,095
- See you in the morning.
- Good night.

123
00:08:18,798 --> 00:08:21,063
Husband, my eye.

124
00:08:21,234 --> 00:08:22,896
She's gonna call
everybody she knows

125
00:08:23,069 --> 00:08:26,597
and tell them that she's on a
first-name basis with a multi-billionaire.

126
00:08:28,808 --> 00:08:32,540
I know that's what I'm gonna
do. Good night, everybody.

127
00:08:34,114 --> 00:08:35,878
See you tomorrow.

128
00:08:36,416 --> 00:08:38,385
So I really fooled you two?

129
00:08:39,753 --> 00:08:41,483
- You fooled me.
- Me too.

130
00:08:41,654 --> 00:08:44,954
Oh, great, I really
fooled you both.

131
00:08:45,125 --> 00:08:47,594
I'm gonna have to get
some frogs to put in your bed.

132
00:08:47,761 --> 00:08:51,630
- Revenge. Heh.
- Oh, that's funny, dad, real funny.

133
00:08:53,700 --> 00:08:55,828
Mr. Gateway, can I come in?

134
00:08:56,703 --> 00:08:59,730
Just wanted to thank
my partner in crime.

135
00:09:34,741 --> 00:09:36,642
Leanne, what's going on?

136
00:09:36,810 --> 00:09:38,711
Robert Gateway's dead.

137
00:09:38,878 --> 00:09:41,677
I mean, really dead.
Somebody stabbed him to death.

138
00:09:41,848 --> 00:09:46,115
- He's in there? I wanna see him?
- No, no. I spoke to the police.

139
00:09:46,286 --> 00:09:48,551
They want me to keep the
room locked until they get here.

140
00:09:48,721 --> 00:09:52,158
- Come on, Leanne. Give us a break.
- I'll bet it's Colonel Mustard.

141
00:09:52,325 --> 00:09:54,521
In the living room
with a candlestick.

142
00:09:54,694 --> 00:09:56,492
This isn't a game. The
guy is dead in there.

143
00:09:56,663 --> 00:09:58,996
He's got a letter opener
sticking out of his chest.

144
00:09:59,165 --> 00:10:01,896
You two work together.
You could be in on it with her.

145
00:10:02,068 --> 00:10:05,129
No. This isn't a hoax.
The man is really dead.

146
00:10:05,305 --> 00:10:08,332
- Sure he is.
- Oh, please. All right.

147
00:10:08,508 --> 00:10:11,205
It's gonna take the police a
couple of hours to get here.

148
00:10:11,377 --> 00:10:13,437
So could we all just
go back to our rooms.

149
00:10:13,613 --> 00:10:17,175
Go on. We'll all take a look
when the police get here.

150
00:10:19,986 --> 00:10:22,854
I think the game has begun.

151
00:10:34,968 --> 00:10:37,528
- Body's in the den.
- Mm-hm.

152
00:10:37,704 --> 00:10:40,503
- Hiya, Ben.
- Pat.

153
00:10:46,980 --> 00:10:49,040
- So where is he?
- Ah.

154
00:10:50,783 --> 00:10:52,615
He's gone.

155
00:10:53,186 --> 00:10:55,815
- Dad!
- Here we go again.

156
00:10:58,024 --> 00:11:00,687
I don't see anyone
murdered here.

157
00:11:00,860 --> 00:11:02,954
Is this another one of those
murder mystery weekends?

158
00:11:03,129 --> 00:11:04,461
Yes, ma'am.

159
00:11:04,631 --> 00:11:07,533
When we first arrived, she
and Mr. Gateway fooled us

160
00:11:07,700 --> 00:11:09,566
into thinking he
had been murdered.

161
00:11:09,736 --> 00:11:14,902
- Heh, looks like they're at it again.
- I don't like people wasting my time.

162
00:11:15,074 --> 00:11:18,067
- Oh, no, no, no. He was dead.
- Oh, no, I... I saw him too.

163
00:11:18,244 --> 00:11:19,906
I checked for pulse.
There was none.

164
00:11:20,079 --> 00:11:22,071
Listen, Leanne, when
you find the dead body,

165
00:11:22,248 --> 00:11:25,116
- tell him I'd like to talk to him.
- I told you...

166
00:11:26,519 --> 00:11:29,819
Wait a minute. Wait a minute!

167
00:11:31,257 --> 00:11:34,193
Now, I didn't see the body.

168
00:11:34,360 --> 00:11:36,591
But I know Leanne wouldn't
have brought you out here

169
00:11:36,763 --> 00:11:38,698
just on a practical joke.

170
00:11:39,432 --> 00:11:40,900
Would you?

171
00:11:41,634 --> 00:11:44,263
- Well, of course not.
- Of course not.

172
00:11:44,437 --> 00:11:46,838
Good. Because the only
thing I hate worse than people

173
00:11:47,006 --> 00:11:52,536
wasting my time is
practical jokes, Ms. Mclntyre.

174
00:11:56,216 --> 00:11:58,481
So aren't you gonna give
us any clues this time?

175
00:12:10,063 --> 00:12:12,794
- Good morning. How'd you sleep?
- I didn't.

176
00:12:12,966 --> 00:12:14,127
Me neither.

177
00:12:14,300 --> 00:12:17,759
Nothing like seeing your
first real live dead body.

178
00:12:17,937 --> 00:12:19,735
Darn it, pop, I know
Robert is dead.

179
00:12:19,906 --> 00:12:21,966
I know one of those
three people killed him.

180
00:12:22,141 --> 00:12:24,269
And I know that the
killer moved the body

181
00:12:24,444 --> 00:12:27,107
in order to postpone
the police investigation.

182
00:12:27,280 --> 00:12:31,149
I am gonna find that body
if it's the last thing I do.

183
00:12:31,317 --> 00:12:33,843
- Morning.
- Whoa, whoa, whoa.

184
00:12:34,020 --> 00:12:35,060
Jane, where are you going?

185
00:12:36,456 --> 00:12:38,084
Out back, to look for clues.

186
00:12:38,591 --> 00:12:41,686
No, no, I told you
already. This isn't a game.

187
00:12:42,362 --> 00:12:44,490
Why don't you go get
Hugo and Elliot and tell them

188
00:12:44,664 --> 00:12:46,656
that we're serving
breakfast in the dining room.

189
00:12:46,833 --> 00:12:49,132
Oh. Okay.

190
00:12:50,103 --> 00:12:53,130
Oh. Dad, need your help.

191
00:12:53,306 --> 00:12:56,140
Need you to regale them with
stories, the longer the better.

192
00:12:56,676 --> 00:13:00,340
That way, Cliff and I will look
for the body with no interruptions.

193
00:13:00,513 --> 00:13:02,607
I just hate it when
you make me do that.

194
00:13:02,782 --> 00:13:05,342
Just regale them,
dad. You do it so well.

195
00:13:05,518 --> 00:13:07,282
What if they don't
want to be regaled?

196
00:13:07,453 --> 00:13:09,479
Regale them, anyway.

197
00:13:12,358 --> 00:13:16,022
It must be a terrible responsibility,
pleading for somebody's life.

198
00:13:16,195 --> 00:13:19,825
Weren't you involved in the
murder of that famous critic?

199
00:13:20,933 --> 00:13:22,799
Oh, yeah.

200
00:13:23,236 --> 00:13:29,870
Ah, well, there was this group
and they were putting on a play.

201
00:13:35,415 --> 00:13:38,715
<i>I never understood why
everybody hated this play.</i>

202
00:13:38,885 --> 00:13:40,717
<i>I loved it.</i>

203
00:13:40,887 --> 00:13:43,379
<i>The play we've all been
waiting for.</i> Lovers and Lawyers

204
00:13:43,556 --> 00:13:47,049
<i>has been directed
haphazardly by Tommy DeLuca,</i>

205
00:13:47,226 --> 00:13:49,127
<i>a has-been who never was.</i>

206
00:13:49,295 --> 00:13:52,094
<i>The production is so tacky</i>

207
00:13:52,265 --> 00:13:57,795
<i>that it can only be produced by
that master of cheap, Guy Palmer.</i>

208
00:13:57,970 --> 00:14:03,170
<i>Written by Sam Spelvin, the script
would have to be greatly improved</i>

209
00:14:03,343 --> 00:14:06,211
<i>to be called amateurish.</i>

210
00:14:06,379 --> 00:14:09,838
<i>John Bosley Hackett was
the most powerful theater critic</i>

211
00:14:10,016 --> 00:14:15,080
<i>in the country, and somebody
really didn't like his review.</i>

212
00:14:35,208 --> 00:14:38,940
The police ended up
arresting the author of the play.

213
00:14:42,382 --> 00:14:47,548
I know I was found over the
body, and I certainly had a motive.

214
00:14:48,654 --> 00:14:50,350
I swear, Ben, I didn't do it.

215
00:14:51,090 --> 00:14:53,616
Okay. Let's assume that's true.

216
00:14:53,793 --> 00:14:56,592
Uh, who knew you were
going up to see Mr. Hackett?

217
00:14:57,296 --> 00:14:58,525
Everybody.

218
00:14:58,698 --> 00:15:01,793
Sally Ann White, Guy
Palmer, Tommy DeLuca.

219
00:15:01,968 --> 00:15:05,132
They all had a lot to
lose if the show failed?

220
00:15:05,304 --> 00:15:07,796
Yes, and they all hated Hackett.

221
00:15:11,878 --> 00:15:15,076
I understand that you
were up here alone

222
00:15:15,248 --> 00:15:17,808
watching rehearsal when
Mr. Hackett was killed.

223
00:15:17,984 --> 00:15:21,079
Yes. That's right.
Well, except for Willie,

224
00:15:21,254 --> 00:15:23,348
follow-spot operator. Willie!

225
00:15:23,823 --> 00:15:26,019
Say hello to Mr. Matlock.

226
00:15:26,192 --> 00:15:28,821
Hello, Willie, heh.

227
00:15:29,829 --> 00:15:32,765
- Well, I enjoyed talking with you.
- My pleasure.

228
00:15:34,133 --> 00:15:35,897
<i>There was one
thing about this case</i>

229
00:15:36,068 --> 00:15:37,900
<i>that kept nagging at me.</i>

230
00:15:38,070 --> 00:15:41,268
<i>It was John Bosley
Hackett's breakfast.</i>

231
00:15:43,543 --> 00:15:46,445
Very nice. A little
awkward, but very nice.

232
00:15:46,612 --> 00:15:49,081
I was just going over
something in the police report.

233
00:15:49,248 --> 00:15:51,615
- What?
- Well, uh,

234
00:15:51,784 --> 00:15:55,050
John Bosley
Hackett's, uh, last meal.

235
00:15:55,221 --> 00:15:58,214
See, he had eaten
the eggs and toast

236
00:15:58,391 --> 00:16:00,883
and the pancake and the
syrup and the grape jelly.

237
00:16:01,060 --> 00:16:03,180
But he hadn't touched the
orange juice and the coffee.

238
00:16:03,329 --> 00:16:05,560
Isn't that going
at it backwards?

239
00:16:05,731 --> 00:16:08,792
Do you think he was killed
because of his bad eating habits?

240
00:16:11,504 --> 00:16:14,838
Uh, Mr. DeLuca, John
Bosley Hackett's review

241
00:16:15,007 --> 00:16:18,637
was not very favorable
for you. Was it?

242
00:16:19,979 --> 00:16:21,709
Personally, I
don't read reviews.

243
00:16:21,881 --> 00:16:27,149
Well, this is a, uh,
15-year-old review.

244
00:16:27,320 --> 00:16:30,415
Uh, it's John Bosley
Hackett's, you see.

245
00:16:30,590 --> 00:16:35,790
And the play was <i>St. Joan of
Rock.</i> You were the director.

246
00:16:35,962 --> 00:16:39,194
He hated the play. And
he hated your direction.

247
00:16:39,966 --> 00:16:44,370
But he saved his most
devastating remarks for the actress.

248
00:16:44,537 --> 00:16:46,005
Do you remember her name?

249
00:16:47,640 --> 00:16:52,010
- Janine Soule.
- Janine Soule DeLuca.

250
00:16:52,178 --> 00:16:53,441
Your bride of six months.

251
00:16:54,413 --> 00:16:58,817
She blamed you for her
horrible review, didn't she?

252
00:16:59,552 --> 00:17:03,546
- Afraid so.
- And that's why she left you.

253
00:17:03,723 --> 00:17:06,192
You could say that.

254
00:17:06,359 --> 00:17:09,625
You were present when
Mr. Hackett told Sam Spelvin

255
00:17:09,795 --> 00:17:13,891
that he was going to review his play,
even though it was only a preview,

256
00:17:14,066 --> 00:17:16,331
- and that he hated it, weren't you?
- Yes. I was.

257
00:17:16,502 --> 00:17:18,630
And you were present the
next morning at the theater

258
00:17:18,804 --> 00:17:23,242
when you learned that Sam
was gonna go talk to Mr. Hackett?

259
00:17:23,409 --> 00:17:25,173
- Yes.
- Would you tell the court

260
00:17:25,344 --> 00:17:26,869
what you did right after that?

261
00:17:28,080 --> 00:17:29,810
Well, I resumed rehearsal.

262
00:17:29,982 --> 00:17:33,009
- And you watched from the balcony?
- Yeah.

263
00:17:33,185 --> 00:17:36,622
I was up there with, uh,
Willie, the follow-spot operator.

264
00:17:36,789 --> 00:17:39,281
I had a long talk with
Willie the other day,

265
00:17:39,458 --> 00:17:42,724
and he tells me that
a follow-spot operator

266
00:17:42,895 --> 00:17:46,297
can't take his eyes off
the stage for a second.

267
00:17:46,465 --> 00:17:50,027
So he can't say whether
you stayed or left.

268
00:17:52,238 --> 00:17:57,176
Uh, Mr. DeLuca, you
wear a hairpiece, don't you?

269
00:17:58,344 --> 00:18:01,109
- I beg your pardon?
- The man who killed Mr. Hackett

270
00:18:01,280 --> 00:18:04,114
attacked him with
a letter opener.

271
00:18:04,283 --> 00:18:07,310
Mr. Hackett tried
to defend himself,

272
00:18:07,486 --> 00:18:11,355
and he grabbed at his murderer
and pulled something off him

273
00:18:11,524 --> 00:18:13,857
that fell into his breakfast.

274
00:18:15,061 --> 00:18:16,222
It was your wig, wasn't it?

275
00:18:17,396 --> 00:18:20,889
You were afraid particles of
your hairpiece would be found

276
00:18:21,067 --> 00:18:24,060
in the eggs, the toast, the
pancakes, the syrup, the grape jelly.

277
00:18:24,236 --> 00:18:26,364
So you flushed it down
the toilet, didn't you?

278
00:18:26,539 --> 00:18:28,599
- No!
- And you rushed back to your room.

279
00:18:29,642 --> 00:18:32,522
I mean, it would have looked very
odd for you to come out of that balcony

280
00:18:32,678 --> 00:18:35,011
with the eggs, the syrup,
the grape jelly in your wig.

281
00:18:35,581 --> 00:18:38,415
So you put on another
one and still got back

282
00:18:38,584 --> 00:18:40,424
before the end of the
dance number, didn't you?

283
00:18:40,953 --> 00:18:42,546
DeLUCA: No.

284
00:18:42,989 --> 00:18:47,893
This first photo is of you

285
00:18:48,060 --> 00:18:51,690
and Sally Ann White and
Guy Palmer and Sam Spelvin

286
00:18:51,864 --> 00:18:54,857
taken just before
the murder, you see.

287
00:18:55,034 --> 00:18:59,699
Your hairpiece is very fashionable.
See how, kind of long over the ears.

288
00:18:59,872 --> 00:19:00,952
DeLUCA: Yes. MATLOCK: Okay.

289
00:19:01,073 --> 00:19:04,771
Now, in this, this newspaper photo,
it was taken as part of the interview

290
00:19:04,944 --> 00:19:09,040
that you gave 20 minutes after
the murder, your hair is shorter.

291
00:19:09,215 --> 00:19:13,050
See that sideburn?
See that? It's shorter.

292
00:19:13,219 --> 00:19:18,624
How'd, heh, your hair get shorter, if
you were just sitting in the balcony?

293
00:19:19,792 --> 00:19:22,694
You put on other
toupee, didn't you?

294
00:19:24,397 --> 00:19:27,333
You're accusing me of
murder, based on a news photo.

295
00:19:27,500 --> 00:19:32,461
Ah, Mr. DeLuca, you
have three hairpieces.

296
00:19:32,638 --> 00:19:35,403
It got me to thinking,
all these hairpieces.

297
00:19:35,574 --> 00:19:38,043
I mean, how do you clean them?

298
00:19:39,278 --> 00:19:41,304
Soap and water.

299
00:19:41,480 --> 00:19:43,506
What if all that
sticky stuff, you know,

300
00:19:44,083 --> 00:19:46,917
wouldn't come out
with soap and water?

301
00:19:47,987 --> 00:19:49,819
I don't know. It never happened.

302
00:19:49,989 --> 00:19:54,324
I think it did. I think you
took it to a wig maker

303
00:19:54,493 --> 00:19:58,294
to have the ruined patches
cut out and rewoven.

304
00:19:58,464 --> 00:19:59,932
You did it the next
day, didn't you?

305
00:20:00,099 --> 00:20:03,934
- No. Check that with my...
- Top Copy. Your wigmaker.

306
00:20:04,103 --> 00:20:06,368
In New York. No. You
wouldn't have done that.

307
00:20:06,539 --> 00:20:08,906
And there aren't that
many wigmakers here.

308
00:20:09,075 --> 00:20:13,171
So it wasn't very
hard to find Mr. Bruce.

309
00:20:14,146 --> 00:20:17,514
He is the person you
took your wig to for repairs,

310
00:20:18,551 --> 00:20:21,146
and he's prepared to testify

311
00:20:21,320 --> 00:20:25,655
that in the ruined
patches of that hairpiece,

312
00:20:25,825 --> 00:20:32,129
he found toast, eggs,
pancakes, syrup,

313
00:20:32,298 --> 00:20:38,067
grape jelly, exactly
Mr. Hackett's last breakfast were.

314
00:20:41,707 --> 00:20:45,303
That was great, heh. You
know, you should write a book.

315
00:20:45,478 --> 00:20:48,744
In fact, we could collaborate.

316
00:20:51,283 --> 00:20:53,582
I know why you're
telling stories.

317
00:20:53,753 --> 00:20:55,688
So your daughter
can get a head start.

318
00:20:55,855 --> 00:20:56,879
Head start on what?

319
00:20:57,056 --> 00:21:00,549
Solving the mystery of
Robert Gateway's death.

320
00:21:07,566 --> 00:21:09,467
There's nobody out there.

321
00:21:10,703 --> 00:21:12,399
Okay. So the room was locked.

322
00:21:12,571 --> 00:21:15,837
The only way the killer could
have gotten the body out of there

323
00:21:16,008 --> 00:21:20,070
was to climb through this
window and toss the body outside.

324
00:21:20,246 --> 00:21:22,738
Oh, yeah. Yeah.
I guess so. Yeah.

325
00:21:22,915 --> 00:21:26,545
So there's gotta be a
body out there somewhere.

326
00:21:26,986 --> 00:21:30,718
- Yeah. Guess so.
- Let's keep searching.

327
00:21:55,414 --> 00:21:57,474
What'd I tell you?

328
00:22:06,425 --> 00:22:09,918
- Oh, my gosh.
- He really is dead.

329
00:22:12,198 --> 00:22:17,432
Oh. You know, let's just all stay
really calm until the police get here.

330
00:22:17,603 --> 00:22:18,798
Again.

331
00:22:18,971 --> 00:22:21,051
Where did you go after
Robert Gateway excused

332
00:22:21,173 --> 00:22:22,751
- himself last night?
- Did my dishes.

333
00:22:22,775 --> 00:22:24,255
- Then I went to bed.
- You did?

334
00:22:24,410 --> 00:22:26,743
- What the hell...
- I didn't see you upstairs.

335
00:22:28,614 --> 00:22:31,209
- No, no, no. No!
- What?

336
00:22:31,383 --> 00:22:36,686
This isn't getting us anywhere.
Like Leanne said, let's just stay calm.

337
00:22:36,856 --> 00:22:38,936
The police will ask the
questions when they get here.

338
00:22:39,091 --> 00:22:42,584
In the meantime, let's just
go in the house and relax.

339
00:22:52,137 --> 00:22:53,196
Poor Robert.

340
00:22:53,372 --> 00:22:56,069
It's just so ironic that he
pretended to be a murder victim

341
00:22:56,242 --> 00:22:58,177
and then wound up
actually being one.

342
00:22:58,344 --> 00:23:02,475
- Yeah. Well, it's happened before.
- Oh, tell them about the con man.

343
00:23:02,648 --> 00:23:04,412
Uh, I think I'll
go wait outside.

344
00:23:04,583 --> 00:23:07,519
- Why?
- Uh, I can guard the body.

345
00:23:07,686 --> 00:23:11,487
- What for?
- I've heard the con man.

346
00:23:13,025 --> 00:23:14,926
- Tell them about the con man.
- Leanne.

347
00:23:15,094 --> 00:23:17,222
Tell them about the con man.

348
00:23:17,396 --> 00:23:18,996
There was this group of con men,

349
00:23:19,164 --> 00:23:21,395
and they were very, very good.

350
00:23:21,567 --> 00:23:22,933
- Oh, my God!
- What is it?

351
00:23:24,837 --> 00:23:26,203
Oh, it's Jason.

352
00:23:32,344 --> 00:23:34,370
Somebody called the police.

353
00:23:34,546 --> 00:23:36,626
- We gotta get outta here.
- We can't leave him here.

354
00:23:36,782 --> 00:23:38,808
Madeline, insider
trading is illegal.

355
00:23:38,984 --> 00:23:40,584
You want the police
to come here find you

356
00:23:40,753 --> 00:23:42,881
standing over Jason's body
with 75 thousand in cash?

357
00:23:43,789 --> 00:23:45,587
Come on. Let's go.

358
00:23:47,726 --> 00:23:48,853
Here. Let me have that.

359
00:23:49,028 --> 00:23:52,226
- What are you doing?
- The police are outside.

360
00:23:52,398 --> 00:23:54,424
We'll come back for it tonight.

361
00:23:54,600 --> 00:23:58,093
<i>They made Brian Davis think
he was gonna make a fortune</i>

362
00:23:58,270 --> 00:24:02,469
<i>in insider trading, when
actually he was gonna be taken</i>

363
00:24:02,641 --> 00:24:04,007
<i>for the ride of his life.</i>

364
00:24:04,176 --> 00:24:05,701
Baby, I wanted to see you.

365
00:24:07,579 --> 00:24:09,172
Okay. Pay attention.

366
00:24:09,348 --> 00:24:12,182
Between 3:30 and 4:00,
you'll bring the mark here.

367
00:24:14,420 --> 00:24:16,548
You conned me.

368
00:24:19,291 --> 00:24:20,384
Yeah. We did.

369
00:24:20,559 --> 00:24:25,759
<i>They already had their next
mark lined up, Kenneth Roberts.</i>

370
00:24:26,465 --> 00:24:28,093
Uh, maybe we
should wait outside.

371
00:24:28,267 --> 00:24:30,634
No, no, no, he's probably
just stepped out from there.

372
00:24:32,304 --> 00:24:34,102
Jason!

373
00:24:37,009 --> 00:24:39,979
Oh, God. Come on,
we gotta get outta here.

374
00:24:46,952 --> 00:24:49,217
There's gotta be cops
outside. What if they see this?

375
00:24:49,388 --> 00:24:52,916
They'll think we killed
them for it. Dump it. Dump it.

376
00:24:56,495 --> 00:24:58,521
Okay, let's go.

377
00:25:02,501 --> 00:25:04,902
What the hell are you
doing? Give me that briefcase.

378
00:25:05,070 --> 00:25:06,561
You want it? Call the police.

379
00:25:07,373 --> 00:25:11,242
This doesn't square things,
Maddy. This is only a down payment.

380
00:25:12,911 --> 00:25:15,847
Jason, get up, we blew it.

381
00:25:16,482 --> 00:25:18,144
Jason.

382
00:25:21,253 --> 00:25:22,619
Jason.

383
00:25:34,366 --> 00:25:35,493
Yes. Give me the police.

384
00:25:37,603 --> 00:25:39,868
They suckered me, Ben.

385
00:25:40,039 --> 00:25:41,166
But I didn't kill Edmunds.

386
00:25:42,908 --> 00:25:45,377
Here's something
else you're gonna like.

387
00:25:45,544 --> 00:25:48,981
Madeline Medford has
turned state's evidence.

388
00:25:49,681 --> 00:25:50,705
What?

389
00:25:51,216 --> 00:25:54,448
She believes you
killed Jason Edmunds.

390
00:25:58,724 --> 00:26:00,488
Well, Bob, you find anything?

391
00:26:01,060 --> 00:26:04,861
No, lot of empty cubicles out
there. Fax machine doesn't work.

392
00:26:05,030 --> 00:26:06,054
It's all show.

393
00:26:09,935 --> 00:26:12,029
- What's all this?
- Edmunds wanted to make it

394
00:26:12,204 --> 00:26:13,763
look like he was
following some stock.

395
00:26:13,939 --> 00:26:15,430
Yeah.

396
00:26:16,108 --> 00:26:19,943
I understand you and
Jason Edmunds were close.

397
00:26:20,112 --> 00:26:23,571
You know what you can do
with your sympathy, Mr. Matlock.

398
00:26:23,749 --> 00:26:26,344
Wrap it up and send
it to the federal prison

399
00:26:26,518 --> 00:26:29,078
where your client will
be serving a life sentence.

400
00:26:29,254 --> 00:26:32,224
You make a business
out of breaking the law

401
00:26:32,391 --> 00:26:36,192
and then get mad when you
think somebody else might have.

402
00:26:36,361 --> 00:26:40,731
Yes. We have a very
firm code of ethics.

403
00:26:40,899 --> 00:26:42,299
We don't kill anybody.

404
00:26:42,468 --> 00:26:45,438
<i>Madeline Medford had an
alibi for the time of the murder,</i>

405
00:26:45,604 --> 00:26:48,403
<i>and I was convinced that
her partner, Jack McCarthy,</i>

406
00:26:48,574 --> 00:26:50,065
<i>was not as ethical
as she thought.</i>

407
00:26:50,242 --> 00:26:53,474
I'm Ben Matlock. This
is Michelle Thomas.

408
00:26:53,645 --> 00:26:57,639
Uh, we'd like to ask you some
questions about Jack McCarthy.

409
00:26:57,816 --> 00:27:02,186
<i>The truth is, the mark was a lot
smarter than anybody thought.</i>

410
00:27:02,354 --> 00:27:05,290
Never had a shot at
easy money before.

411
00:27:05,924 --> 00:27:08,553
Jack McCarthy said that
you and he were together

412
00:27:08,727 --> 00:27:13,131
for more than two hours before
you visited, uh, Jason Edmunds.

413
00:27:13,599 --> 00:27:15,227
Yeah. I think so,

414
00:27:15,400 --> 00:27:17,369
except when I went to
the bank to get the money.

415
00:27:17,536 --> 00:27:19,971
McCarthy didn't go with you?
- No.

416
00:27:20,839 --> 00:27:24,139
No. We met back here before
we went over to the office.

417
00:27:25,878 --> 00:27:27,870
Now, Mr. Roberts,

418
00:27:28,046 --> 00:27:31,346
do you remember the day
you met, uh, Jack McCarthy?

419
00:27:31,517 --> 00:27:33,577
I was jogging.

420
00:27:33,886 --> 00:27:37,516
His car broke down.
I gave him a lift.

421
00:27:38,090 --> 00:27:40,525
Yeah. But what about this?

422
00:27:41,226 --> 00:27:43,855
What if you drove up there

423
00:27:44,029 --> 00:27:46,658
to find Jack McCarthy?

424
00:27:46,832 --> 00:27:48,164
You knew who he was, didn't you?

425
00:27:51,336 --> 00:27:53,828
Well, well, that's not
true. Why would I do that?

426
00:27:54,006 --> 00:27:55,030
For two reasons.

427
00:27:55,541 --> 00:27:59,478
One, to get to Jason Edmunds,
that's the man you were after.

428
00:27:59,645 --> 00:28:02,171
Two, to give yourself
a perfect alibi.

429
00:28:03,682 --> 00:28:06,208
On the day of the murder,
you went to the bank

430
00:28:06,385 --> 00:28:10,755
and withdrew 20 thousand
dollars to give to Jack McCarthy.

431
00:28:10,923 --> 00:28:12,915
- Right? Okay.
- That's right.

432
00:28:13,625 --> 00:28:18,620
Now, I'm showing you this
document which we subpoenaed from

433
00:28:18,797 --> 00:28:23,531
the Atlanta Crestmoor National
Bank and ask you if you recognize it.

434
00:28:23,702 --> 00:28:24,965
Ah.

435
00:28:26,038 --> 00:28:27,631
Yeah, that's my bank statement.

436
00:28:27,806 --> 00:28:29,741
It shows the 20 thousand
dollar withdrawal.

437
00:28:29,908 --> 00:28:33,777
But not on the day of the
murder. See, look, right here.

438
00:28:34,313 --> 00:28:37,977
The money was
withdrawn on March 17th.

439
00:28:38,150 --> 00:28:40,676
The day before the murder, heh.

440
00:28:41,954 --> 00:28:43,582
That must be a mistake.

441
00:28:43,755 --> 00:28:47,658
In Jason Edmunds' office,
there's a computer monitor

442
00:28:47,826 --> 00:28:52,127
with a lot of symbols
and initials on it.

443
00:28:52,297 --> 00:28:54,097
Well, they're New York
Stock Exchange symbols.

444
00:28:54,233 --> 00:28:56,361
That's why I knew
he was legitimate.

445
00:28:56,535 --> 00:28:58,970
Right. Right. Look
here right at the bottom.

446
00:28:59,137 --> 00:29:00,662
See that word.
See these initials.

447
00:29:00,839 --> 00:29:02,865
"PMR."

448
00:29:03,041 --> 00:29:04,907
See there. Your son's name?

449
00:29:06,111 --> 00:29:10,515
- Peter.
- Peter Michael Roberts.

450
00:29:10,682 --> 00:29:11,980
PMR.

451
00:29:12,150 --> 00:29:14,415
That's just a
coincidence. It's...

452
00:29:15,821 --> 00:29:19,758
Mr. Roberts, how is your son?

453
00:29:21,493 --> 00:29:23,428
He passed away.

454
00:29:23,595 --> 00:29:25,427
He committed suicide, didn't he?

455
00:29:26,031 --> 00:29:30,492
He committed suicide because
he had lost thousands of dollars

456
00:29:30,669 --> 00:29:34,436
to a con man, Jason Edmunds.

457
00:29:35,340 --> 00:29:38,742
Your son's death
broke your heart.

458
00:29:41,713 --> 00:29:43,375
Mr. Roberts,

459
00:29:45,350 --> 00:29:50,516
this is the police photograph
of the murder scene.

460
00:29:50,689 --> 00:29:54,524
At first, nothing seems unusual.

461
00:29:55,193 --> 00:29:56,889
But if you look
at his right wrist,

462
00:29:57,062 --> 00:30:01,397
what do you suppose caused
that white line on his wrist?

463
00:30:01,566 --> 00:30:05,230
- Well, how would I know?
- Con men like to steal

464
00:30:05,404 --> 00:30:08,806
something personal
off of people they con.

465
00:30:08,974 --> 00:30:13,241
And Mr. McCarthy and I spent
a good deal of time talking.

466
00:30:13,412 --> 00:30:17,281
And he is now willing to testify

467
00:30:19,251 --> 00:30:21,243
that Jason Edmunds

468
00:30:21,420 --> 00:30:24,481
took something
personal off your son,

469
00:30:25,324 --> 00:30:27,953
a gold ID bracelet.

470
00:30:30,162 --> 00:30:33,132
And on the back was inscribed,

471
00:30:35,000 --> 00:30:41,372
"You have made me so
proud." Then the word, "Dad."

472
00:30:44,943 --> 00:30:48,539
Mr. Roberts, I noticed that
you're wearing an ID bracelet.

473
00:30:48,714 --> 00:30:50,478
Would you mind taking it off?

474
00:31:05,931 --> 00:31:07,797
Those con men

475
00:31:07,966 --> 00:31:09,628
swindled him out of everything,

476
00:31:09,801 --> 00:31:15,468
savings, a house, car.

477
00:31:15,640 --> 00:31:18,838
He was so ashamed.
He couldn't tell me.

478
00:31:20,178 --> 00:31:22,909
I found out when I...
When I read the letter

479
00:31:23,081 --> 00:31:26,074
that was lying next to his body.

480
00:31:40,699 --> 00:31:43,669
We gotta stop meeting
like this. People will talk.

481
00:31:43,835 --> 00:31:45,701
- You saw the body?
- Mm-hm.

482
00:31:45,871 --> 00:31:48,864
Folks, while the medical
examiner is doing his thing out there,

483
00:31:49,040 --> 00:31:51,236
I'm gonna need statements
from each of you in private.

484
00:31:51,410 --> 00:31:53,402
Who wants to go first?

485
00:31:54,546 --> 00:31:57,243
- He would.
- Buzz off.

486
00:31:58,784 --> 00:32:02,277
Well, you seem anxious
to talk. We'll take you.

487
00:32:02,454 --> 00:32:05,322
I'm freezing. Could I go
upstairs and get a sweater?

488
00:32:05,490 --> 00:32:09,552
Sure. Only make it
snappy. Come on.

489
00:32:55,040 --> 00:32:57,566
This was just
found near the body.

490
00:32:58,310 --> 00:33:01,337
And it's not from anything
Mr. Gateway had on.

491
00:33:01,513 --> 00:33:02,776
Anybody recognize it?

492
00:33:02,948 --> 00:33:04,678
- Not my style.
- Not mine.

493
00:33:04,850 --> 00:33:08,480
Well, it's possible that this came
off of something the murderer had on.

494
00:33:08,653 --> 00:33:11,088
So people, we're going to
have to go through the clothes

495
00:33:11,256 --> 00:33:14,055
you brought with
you. Any objections?

496
00:33:14,826 --> 00:33:17,261
Good. Shouldn't take long.

497
00:33:18,330 --> 00:33:21,562
- Well, I'm getting kind of hungry.
- Me too.

498
00:33:21,733 --> 00:33:23,964
Sit down. I'll go see what
there is in the kitchen.

499
00:33:43,054 --> 00:33:45,819
Why, Jane, what have you
got there? An edible sweater?

500
00:33:45,991 --> 00:33:47,653
Let go off me.

501
00:33:47,826 --> 00:33:49,818
It wouldn't happen to
be missing a button now?

502
00:33:49,995 --> 00:33:52,521
- Let go.
- I've got her.

503
00:33:53,698 --> 00:33:58,398
I've got her. I've got
her. I've got the killer.

504
00:34:08,246 --> 00:34:12,240
When I realized it came
from my sweater, I panicked.

505
00:34:12,417 --> 00:34:15,251
All I could think to do
was to try and get rid of it.

506
00:34:15,420 --> 00:34:17,082
Don't you see?

507
00:34:17,255 --> 00:34:20,054
Whoever killed Robert
planted that button there

508
00:34:20,225 --> 00:34:21,887
to make it look
like I had killed him.

509
00:34:22,060 --> 00:34:24,188
Where were you after
dinner last night, Mrs. Kravitz?

510
00:34:24,930 --> 00:34:25,954
In my room.

511
00:34:26,131 --> 00:34:27,155
- Alone?
- Yes.

512
00:34:27,332 --> 00:34:30,769
That's not true.
She was in my room.

513
00:34:30,936 --> 00:34:34,896
In fact, she was there
pretty much all night.

514
00:34:35,073 --> 00:34:38,100
Is there something going
on here we don't know about?

515
00:34:40,612 --> 00:34:42,137
We're lovers.

516
00:34:42,314 --> 00:34:46,911
We came here to spend the weekend
together, not to solve some mystery.

517
00:34:47,085 --> 00:34:49,645
Yeah? Well, I think they're
covering for each other.

518
00:34:49,821 --> 00:34:51,722
And I think you're the murderer.
- Okay.

519
00:34:51,890 --> 00:34:54,917
Look, uh, as long
as you're all here,

520
00:34:55,093 --> 00:34:56,994
I'm gonna need a set of
prints from each of you.

521
00:34:57,162 --> 00:35:00,098
So if you all just sit
down, we'll get started.

522
00:35:00,265 --> 00:35:03,531
- Only prints you'll need are his.
- Shut up.

523
00:35:04,603 --> 00:35:08,870
Ah, Dad, how about the
Dan Galloway murder case?

524
00:35:09,040 --> 00:35:11,805
Dad! The talk show
host that got killed?

525
00:35:11,977 --> 00:35:13,937
Why don't you tell that
story? That's a great one.

526
00:35:14,079 --> 00:35:15,604
Oh, I like that
one. Tell that one.

527
00:35:15,780 --> 00:35:19,581
- Cliff. LEANNE: Come
on, it's a great story.

528
00:35:21,486 --> 00:35:25,480
<i>Dan Galloway had the hottest
daytime talk show on television.</i>

529
00:35:25,657 --> 00:35:27,148
<i>Women in power,</i>

530
00:35:27,325 --> 00:35:30,818
<i>from Cleopatra to Imelda Marcos,
has long been an issue for debate,</i>

531
00:35:30,996 --> 00:35:33,329
<i>brains versus breasts.</i>

532
00:35:33,498 --> 00:35:36,866
<i>That's the focus of this
edition of Dan Galloway.</i>

533
00:35:37,035 --> 00:35:39,732
<i>He had a knack for
pushing people's buttons.</i>

534
00:35:40,739 --> 00:35:41,900
<i>Judge Cordante.</i>

535
00:35:42,073 --> 00:35:45,373
<i>Here she is with
Governor Leon Griese,</i>

536
00:35:45,543 --> 00:35:48,672
<i>now with Congressman
Robert Arnold,</i>

537
00:35:48,847 --> 00:35:51,681
<i>and here with state party
chairman Edward Dorfman.</i>

538
00:35:51,850 --> 00:35:54,945
<i>Would you not assume that
she's offering these gentlemen</i>

539
00:35:55,120 --> 00:35:57,851
<i>more than professional opinions?</i>

540
00:35:58,023 --> 00:36:01,391
Those were pictures taken
at various political events.

541
00:36:01,559 --> 00:36:03,323
You've used them out of context.

542
00:36:03,495 --> 00:36:05,225
And I'm not going to
stay here any longer

543
00:36:05,397 --> 00:36:07,229
and dignify this with an answer.

544
00:36:07,399 --> 00:36:09,664
And, uh, now, a word
from our sponsor.

545
00:36:09,834 --> 00:36:11,234
And we're in commercial.

546
00:36:12,604 --> 00:36:14,402
- Everybody stand by.
- Marla.

547
00:36:14,572 --> 00:36:15,938
Marla.

548
00:36:16,307 --> 00:36:19,209
Baby, you were great. Your
decision to storm off, brilliant.

549
00:36:19,878 --> 00:36:22,177
You creep. You
made a fool out of me.

550
00:36:22,347 --> 00:36:24,680
I made a personality out of
you and I'll invite you back.

551
00:36:24,849 --> 00:36:27,045
You'll hear from my attorney.

552
00:36:29,287 --> 00:36:32,746
Welcome back. Katherine. Leanne.

553
00:36:32,924 --> 00:36:36,622
How do you answer to your
reputation as dragon ladies?

554
00:36:37,228 --> 00:36:39,663
That's why I chose to
leave the television business.

555
00:36:39,831 --> 00:36:44,462
Leanne, didn't your sudden
decision to devote your life

556
00:36:44,636 --> 00:36:47,868
to coordinating napkins
with centerpieces come about

557
00:36:48,039 --> 00:36:50,838
because your reputation
made it impossible

558
00:36:51,009 --> 00:36:53,137
for you to get another
job in television?

559
00:36:53,778 --> 00:36:57,476
My decision was based
on the fact that I was set up.

560
00:36:57,649 --> 00:37:02,383
My dear, we're all being set up,
just to get an extra rating point.

561
00:37:02,554 --> 00:37:05,888
Well, is that any worse than
ridiculing people for not being invited

562
00:37:06,057 --> 00:37:07,286
to some society party

563
00:37:07,459 --> 00:37:11,920
and then slashing those who were
for wearing the same dress twice?

564
00:37:12,597 --> 00:37:15,431
Careful, Katherine, move
too fast, your face might fall.

565
00:37:18,937 --> 00:37:20,657
- And we're in commercial.
- Thank you.

566
00:37:20,805 --> 00:37:22,485
That's should be good
for another 41 share.

567
00:37:22,640 --> 00:37:24,836
<i>Being Dan Galloway's producer</i>

568
00:37:25,009 --> 00:37:27,444
<i>was a nightmare
for Lisa Bateman.</i>

569
00:37:27,612 --> 00:37:29,372
None of our stations
are gonna run that show.

570
00:37:30,415 --> 00:37:33,078
It's controversy, it's heat.

571
00:37:33,251 --> 00:37:34,931
The public eats it up.
Look at the ratings.

572
00:37:35,086 --> 00:37:39,182
It was crap, slanderous
crap and I'm not gonna run it.

573
00:37:39,357 --> 00:37:40,655
Oh.

574
00:37:41,526 --> 00:37:46,464
Poor Lisa, so
refined. So tasteful.

575
00:37:46,631 --> 00:37:48,065
Much too good
for Dan. All right?

576
00:37:48,900 --> 00:37:50,095
I never said that.

577
00:37:52,137 --> 00:37:56,370
I bought you a present. Open it.

578
00:37:58,810 --> 00:38:00,779
What's the occasion?

579
00:38:08,353 --> 00:38:11,983
- Don't you get it?
- I'm giving you the ax.

580
00:38:54,299 --> 00:38:59,135
Well, they got a pretty
good case against you.

581
00:38:59,304 --> 00:39:01,830
You were in the building
at the time of the murder.

582
00:39:02,006 --> 00:39:04,168
Your fingerprints are all
over the murder weapon.

583
00:39:04,976 --> 00:39:08,936
Ben, the only time I saw that ax
was in his office, and I left it there.

584
00:39:10,114 --> 00:39:11,594
Who else could
have gotten hold of it?

585
00:39:12,116 --> 00:39:13,584
Anyone who works in the studio.

586
00:39:13,751 --> 00:39:15,549
Anyone who had a
pass to the building today.

587
00:39:15,720 --> 00:39:17,760
The three women who were
on the show this afternoon.

588
00:39:17,922 --> 00:39:20,448
They were all angry
enough to want to kill him.

589
00:39:21,759 --> 00:39:23,489
- Find anything?
- Not much.

590
00:39:23,661 --> 00:39:25,926
Matches, the top of a pen.

591
00:39:26,097 --> 00:39:28,577
All of it typical of what you'd
find on the floor of an office.

592
00:39:31,603 --> 00:39:33,401
- They tracing it?
- According to the police,

593
00:39:33,571 --> 00:39:36,063
that button wasn't found on any
of the clothing Joe was wearing

594
00:39:36,241 --> 00:39:38,335
- the day of the murder.
- Huh.

595
00:39:38,509 --> 00:39:41,069
Can you rewind that part?

596
00:39:42,247 --> 00:39:44,273
Stop! That's it.

597
00:39:44,449 --> 00:39:47,078
That's it. It's the bracelet.

598
00:39:47,252 --> 00:39:49,721
We're trying to track
down this bracelet?

599
00:39:49,888 --> 00:39:51,618
Do you recognize it?

600
00:39:52,257 --> 00:39:54,692
Of course, that's an
Andrea Claggett design.

601
00:39:54,859 --> 00:39:58,125
Do you know if this button
came from that bracelet?

602
00:39:59,297 --> 00:40:00,959
This button is brass.

603
00:40:01,132 --> 00:40:04,591
Andrea Claggett uses
only precious metals.

604
00:40:04,769 --> 00:40:09,036
Now, if the killer raised the ax...
You be the killer. You be the killer.

605
00:40:09,207 --> 00:40:10,869
- Okay. Now, he...
- She.

606
00:40:11,042 --> 00:40:15,070
She, yeah. She...
She hit him, um,

607
00:40:15,246 --> 00:40:17,442
and he fell

608
00:40:18,449 --> 00:40:24,753
Probably something like that.
Now, that's when he left the clue.

609
00:40:24,923 --> 00:40:31,727
He got up and he
programmed the keyboard.

610
00:40:31,896 --> 00:40:35,628
And then he pulled the plug.

611
00:40:35,800 --> 00:40:40,397
Now, can you run the tape and stop
it where he programmed it to stop?

612
00:40:40,571 --> 00:40:41,937
Sure.

613
00:40:52,216 --> 00:40:54,981
<i>- Next.</i>
- Of course.

614
00:40:55,420 --> 00:41:00,017
I'm done. Of course,
that's it. That's it.

615
00:41:00,892 --> 00:41:03,862
When Judge Cordante's
picture appeared on the monitor,

616
00:41:04,028 --> 00:41:08,159
we thought he was trying to
tell us that she had killed him.

617
00:41:08,333 --> 00:41:11,792
But then we have to remember,
he was dying. He was on the floor.

618
00:41:11,970 --> 00:41:15,839
He was looking at the
monitor upside down.

619
00:41:16,007 --> 00:41:18,442
And when he pulled that plug,

620
00:41:19,177 --> 00:41:25,276
this digital counter
stopped at 337.

621
00:41:26,050 --> 00:41:32,422
But if you look at it upside down
the way he did, it spells L-E-E.

622
00:41:33,992 --> 00:41:35,426
You see that, Leanne?

623
00:41:35,593 --> 00:41:38,961
All this means is that he was trying
to set me up again, and, besides,

624
00:41:39,130 --> 00:41:41,656
at the time he was killed, I
was already back in Bakersfield.

625
00:41:41,833 --> 00:41:44,462
No. Thank you.

626
00:41:46,704 --> 00:41:49,401
You checked out of
the motel 6 o'clock.

627
00:41:50,508 --> 00:41:56,709
But you left your car in the
motel garage until almost 8:00.

628
00:41:56,881 --> 00:41:59,146
That places you at
the scene of the crime.

629
00:41:59,317 --> 00:42:03,584
And before you say I have no
proof, I want to take a shot at it.

630
00:42:06,057 --> 00:42:07,821
This button

631
00:42:08,626 --> 00:42:11,653
was found on the
floor of the editing room

632
00:42:11,829 --> 00:42:13,388
where Dan Galloway was killed.

633
00:42:13,564 --> 00:42:16,796
But it doesn't seem
to belong to anybody.

634
00:42:16,968 --> 00:42:20,336
It doesn't seem to
come of anything.

635
00:42:20,505 --> 00:42:25,500
When you came down to do the
taping, you were wearing a jacket.

636
00:42:25,676 --> 00:42:27,804
But then when you
actually did the taping,

637
00:42:27,979 --> 00:42:30,972
you weren't wearing your jacket,
some sort of sound problem.

638
00:42:32,183 --> 00:42:35,176
I wore my red jacket. It
doesn't have any brass buttons.

639
00:42:35,353 --> 00:42:36,878
Bailiff.

640
00:42:37,789 --> 00:42:41,920
This is a rack

641
00:42:42,760 --> 00:42:44,854
of Ms. Giovanni's jackets.

642
00:42:45,897 --> 00:42:48,298
As you can see,

643
00:42:49,133 --> 00:42:52,729
the inside buttons are brass,

644
00:42:53,738 --> 00:42:58,108
just like this button that
was found on the floor

645
00:42:58,276 --> 00:43:00,871
by Dan Galloway's body.

646
00:43:01,913 --> 00:43:06,248
Now, you recognize
this jacket, don't you?

647
00:43:06,417 --> 00:43:07,612
It's yours, isn't it?

648
00:43:07,785 --> 00:43:11,119
I remember spilling coffee
on the sleeve the other day.

649
00:43:13,291 --> 00:43:17,490
But your inside button
doesn't match the brass button.

650
00:43:17,662 --> 00:43:22,191
No. I distinctly remember. I
lost the button last October.

651
00:43:22,366 --> 00:43:26,462
Then why did you wait till the
day after Dan Galloway was killed

652
00:43:26,637 --> 00:43:30,404
to take your coat to
the Forest Dry Cleaners

653
00:43:30,575 --> 00:43:34,205
in Bakersfield and have
them replace this button?

654
00:43:34,378 --> 00:43:36,472
The cleaner's mark's
still inside the jacket.

655
00:43:36,647 --> 00:43:39,048
And we talked to the management.

656
00:43:43,688 --> 00:43:47,557
Would you like to
retain an attorney?

657
00:43:48,392 --> 00:43:51,658
All right. Press firmly.

658
00:43:52,730 --> 00:43:54,198
Thank you.

659
00:43:58,035 --> 00:43:59,970
I love that story.

660
00:44:00,138 --> 00:44:03,302
Would you excuse us
for just a minute, please?

661
00:44:03,474 --> 00:44:08,310
- Cliff, could you help me?
- Um, I really did love it.

662
00:44:10,148 --> 00:44:11,548
Where are they going?

663
00:44:14,986 --> 00:44:17,148
What are you doing?
It's a good sweater.

664
00:44:17,321 --> 00:44:19,813
- He left us a clue.
- He did? Where?

665
00:44:19,991 --> 00:44:22,187
This was in his hand
when I found him.

666
00:44:22,360 --> 00:44:26,161
- And it was tuned to that channel.
- Smoke, but it's just smoke.

667
00:44:26,330 --> 00:44:28,822
That's why I know it's a clue.

668
00:44:30,134 --> 00:44:33,969
One. Seven. Three.
One Seven Three.

669
00:44:34,138 --> 00:44:37,973
The number. One Seven Three.
Just like in the Galloway case.

670
00:44:38,809 --> 00:44:41,244
"E-L-I". Eli.

671
00:44:41,779 --> 00:44:44,840
Eli. Eli. As in Elliot.

672
00:44:45,016 --> 00:44:48,111
I think we'd better go
talk to Detective Jordan.

673
00:44:51,622 --> 00:44:56,219
Mr. Ross, could you tell
me what this is, please?

674
00:44:57,028 --> 00:44:59,148
Well, we just found it in
the lining of your suitcase.

675
00:44:59,297 --> 00:45:01,027
How very James Bond.

676
00:45:01,632 --> 00:45:03,352
Looks like some
kind of binoculars.

677
00:45:03,501 --> 00:45:05,561
No. Not binoculars.
Virtual reality headgear.

678
00:45:07,138 --> 00:45:09,767
- Say again.
- Virtual reality.

679
00:45:09,941 --> 00:45:12,137
It's, uh, you put on these
special glasses and then,

680
00:45:12,310 --> 00:45:14,939
you know, instead of
interacting with reality,

681
00:45:15,112 --> 00:45:21,609
you're interacting with this kind of
computer-generated image reality.

682
00:45:21,786 --> 00:45:24,779
Robert Gateway was rumored
to have been working on a way

683
00:45:24,956 --> 00:45:28,723
to make it into a
mass-marketable toy.

684
00:45:28,893 --> 00:45:31,021
Guess the rumors were true.

685
00:45:31,195 --> 00:45:33,460
I don't know how that
thing got in my suitcase.

686
00:45:33,631 --> 00:45:36,829
I've never seen it before in my
life. They're trying to frame me.

687
00:45:37,001 --> 00:45:38,594
Ask them how it got there.

688
00:45:38,769 --> 00:45:40,931
You know, my
nephew's a stockbroker.

689
00:45:41,105 --> 00:45:44,337
He's always using these
weird terms, like a put and a call.

690
00:45:44,508 --> 00:45:47,740
What's a put and
what's a call, anyway?

691
00:45:47,912 --> 00:45:49,244
What is this, a test?

692
00:45:49,413 --> 00:45:51,973
What's the difference between
selling long and selling short?

693
00:45:54,318 --> 00:45:55,878
Come on, Elliot,
boxing the stock,

694
00:45:56,020 --> 00:45:57,420
what does that mean?

695
00:45:57,588 --> 00:45:58,954
I don't have to take this.

696
00:46:00,791 --> 00:46:03,454
You're not a
stockbroker, are you?

697
00:46:03,861 --> 00:46:05,853
Or a mystery buff.

698
00:46:06,530 --> 00:46:10,831
You're an industrial spy and
came here to steal those plans.

699
00:46:11,002 --> 00:46:13,767
But you wound up committing
murder as well, didn't you?

700
00:46:13,938 --> 00:46:15,406
No.

701
00:46:15,573 --> 00:46:20,102
Well, you see this smudge,
Mr. Ross? I'm pretty sure it's blood.

702
00:46:20,278 --> 00:46:23,180
And if it turns out to be
Robert Gateway's blood,

703
00:46:23,848 --> 00:46:25,840
- you're in a lot of
trouble ELLIOT: Bull!

704
00:46:26,017 --> 00:46:28,851
That doesn't prove I killed him.
- Oh, I'm afraid it does

705
00:46:29,020 --> 00:46:33,890
because if you look very closely,
you'll see that it's not only a smudge,

706
00:46:34,058 --> 00:46:35,287
there's a fingerprint in it.

707
00:46:40,164 --> 00:46:41,792
Your fingerprint.

708
00:46:45,036 --> 00:46:48,336
Well, Detective Jordan,

709
00:46:49,340 --> 00:46:51,366
looks like you
solved this mystery.

710
00:46:51,542 --> 00:46:55,809
Uh, actually, Leanne solved it.

711
00:46:55,980 --> 00:47:00,816
Heh, well, actually, Robert Gateway
solved it with his dying breath.

712
00:47:00,985 --> 00:47:02,613
